% =============================================================================
% REFERENCE LIBRARY
% =============================================================================
%
% ZOTERO INTEGRATION — how to populate this file from your reference manager
%
% Option A — One-time export:
%   1. In Zotero, select your library or a collection
%   2. File → Export Library (or right-click collection → Export Collection)
%   3. Format: Better BibLaTeX   (requires the Better BibTeX plugin — see below)
%   4. Save the file as reference_library.bib and replace this file
%   5. In Overleaf: upload the new .bib via the file tree on the left
%
% Option B — Continuous sync (recommended for active writing):
%   1. Install the Better BibTeX plugin for Zotero:
%        https://retorque.re/zotero-better-bibtex/
%   2. Right-click your Zotero library or collection → Export Collection
%      Format: Better BibLaTeX  |  tick "Keep updated"
%   3. Zotero will keep the exported .bib file up to date automatically.
%      Re-upload the file to Overleaf each time you add new references.
%
% Option C — Overleaf native Zotero sync (not recommended - doesn't allow you to adjust formatting):
%   In Overleaf: Menu → Sync → Zotero
%   This requires a linked Zotero account (set up under Overleaf account settings).
%   Overleaf imports your entire Zotero library as a .bib file automatically.
%
% CITATION KEYS
%   After exporting, each entry has a citation key (e.g. smith_title_2024).
%   This key is shown in the "Citation Key" column in Zotero (visible after
%   installing Better BibTeX). Use it to cite in text: \cite{smith_title_2024}
%
% CITATION STYLE
%   This template uses Vancouver-style superscript numbers.
%   \cite{key} inserts a superscript number and adds the reference to the
%   current chapter's reference list.
%
% =============================================================================


